<article class="env-corollary" id="05CY">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/05CY">Corollary <span data-tag="05CY">4.8.7.8</span>.</a> Let $f: X \rightarrow Z$ be a morphism of Kan complexes and let $n$ be an integer. Then $f$ factors as a composition $X \xrightarrow {f'} Y \xrightarrow {f''} Z$, where $f''$ is $(n-1)$-truncated and $f'$ is $n$-connective. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      Using Theorem <a data-tag="05CS" href="/tag/05CS">4.8.7.3</a>, we can factor $f$ as a composition $f'' \circ f'$, where $f'': \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow Z$ is an $n$-faithful functor of $\infty $-categories and $f': X \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is categorically $n$-connective. If $n \leq 0$, then $f''$ induces an equivalence from $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ to a summand of $Z$, so that $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is a Kan complex. If $n &gt; 0$, Remark <a data-tag="05C8" href="/tag/05C8">4.8.5.13</a> guarantees that $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is a Kan complex. Setting $Y = \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, we observe that $f''$ is $(n-1)$-truncated (Example <a data-tag="05AZ" href="/tag/05AZ">4.8.3.14</a>) and $f'$ is $n$-connective (Example <a data-tag="05C1" href="/tag/05C1">4.8.5.3</a>). 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</article>
